          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What counts as insomnia rather than just a few bad nights?",
          "answer": "The reference material I searched does not say how many nights, or for how long, make sleep trouble count as insomnia. I can't give you that threshold from these sources, so I won't guess at one.\n\nThe \"Insomnia\" summary (condition-insomnia, General clinical reference) does say that chronic insomnia is linked to daytime problems, lower quality of life, and a higher risk of depression. It also says cognitive behavioral therapy is the first-line treatment.\n\nThe same summary lists signs that call for further assessment beyond insomnia treatment:\n- Loud snoring with pauses in breathing that someone else has seen\n- Excessive daytime sleepiness\n- Falling asleep while driving\n- Acting out dreams\n- Sleep trouble alongside low mood or hopelessness\n\nIf you have any of these, or your poor sleep is lasting or affecting your day, a clinician can tell you whether it fits the usual definition of insomnia. Bring a note of when your sleep is poor and for how long.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
